# Project: Integrated Auto Repair Shop System

## Domain Context
This is a **monolithic layered backend** for an auto repair shop. The system manages service orders, customers, vehicles, parts/supplies inventory, and administrative operations.

## Core Domain Entities
- **Customer** — identified uniquely by CPF (individual) or CNPJ (company)
- **Vehicle** — license plate, brand, model, year; always linked to a Customer
- **Service** — available services (oil change, alignment, etc.) with name, description, unit price
- **Part (Peca)** — parts and supplies with stock control
- **Service Order (OS)** — central aggregate linking Customer, Vehicle, Services, Parts; has a lifecycle of statuses
- **Budget** — budget auto-generated from services and parts on a service order
- **User** — system user account; stores name, email, password hash, and roles (ADMIN, ATTENDANT, MECHANIC, CLIENT)
- **Customer** — customer-specific data (CPF/CNPJ, company name, phone), linked 1:1 to a User

## User & Customer Split
- Creating a Customer automatically creates a linked User with role `CLIENT`
- User entity stores: name, email, password hash, roles
- Customer entity stores: CPF/CNPJ, company name, phone, FK to User
- Default password = CPF (individual) or CNPJ (company), hashed with bcrypt
- Deleting a Customer must also delete or deactivate the associated User

## RBAC — Role-Based Access Control
Four roles: `ADMIN`, `ATTENDANT`, `MECHANIC`, `CLIENT`
- Users may hold multiple roles simultaneously (many-to-many via `USER_ROLE`), **except** `ADMIN` which is exclusive
- **ADMIN**: unrestricted access to all endpoints, user management, and role assignment
- **ATTENDANT**: customers, vehicles, service orders, budgets, reports — no user management
- **MECHANIC**: read-only service orders, add services/parts to service orders, status transitions only
- **CLIENT**: own service order tracking + own budget approve/reject; authenticated by email + password; scoped by `customerId` in JWT
- Only `ADMIN` can create other `ADMIN` users or change any user's roles
- Out-of-scope access → HTTP 403

## Service Order Status Lifecycle (ordered, no skipping)
1. `RECEIVED`
2. `IN_DIAGNOSIS`
3. `AWAITING_APPROVAL`
4. `IN_PROGRESS`
5. `COMPLETED`
6. `DELIVERED`

A rejected budget (`REJECTED`) closes the service order without execution.

## Architecture
- Monolithic layered architecture (Controller → Service → Repository)
- PostgreSQL database (ACID, MVCC, JSONB support)
- RESTful APIs documented with Swagger/OpenAPI
- JWT authentication; payload includes `roles` array and `customerId` (when applicable)
- Public endpoints: `POST /auth/login`, `GET /os/:id/status`
- Docker + docker-compose for full environment orchestration

## Non-functional Requirements
- Minimum 90% test coverage on critical domains: service orders, stock, budget
- Structured logs for OS creation, status transitions, validation errors
- Sensitive data (CPF, CNPJ) must never appear in logs or error responses
- Passwords hashed with bcrypt (min cost 12)
- ACID transactions for concurrent OS updates and stock decrements
- API response time under 2 seconds under normal load
- Authorization middleware must not make extra DB calls — rely on JWT payload

## Commit Messages — Conventional Commits
All commits must follow the [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) specification.

### Format
```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

### Types
- `feat` — new feature
- `fix` — bug fix
- `refactor` — code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
- `test` — adding or updating tests
- `chore` — build, tooling, dependency updates, CI changes
- `docs` — documentation only
- `perf` — performance improvement
- `style` — formatting, missing semicolons, etc (no logic change)
- `revert` — revert a previous commit

### Scopes (domain-aligned)
`service-order`, `customer`, `vehicle`, `service`, `part`, `stock`, `budget`, `auth`, `user`, `infra`, `db`, `api`

### Rules
- Subject line max 72 characters, lowercase, no period at end
- Use imperative mood: "add feature" not "added feature"
- Breaking changes must include `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer or `!` after type: `feat(auth)!: change JWT payload structure`
- Reference issues in footer: `Closes #123`
